   \item The uncoordinated benchmark was redefined. The submitted comparison let distribution networks export into a transmission network that has no load of its own; the revised benchmark gives the uncoordinated arrangements a no-reverse-flow interface rule --- today's connection practice --- and reports, separately, that without any interface rule the transmission network cannot accept the distribution exchange in 1 of 12 (passive) and 8 of 12 (price-taker) representative blocks (Sections~3.6 and~4.4).
   \item The limitations section is new: set-valued interface duals with large storage and high flexibility prices; transmission solver recoveries; a $10^{-5}$~p.u.\ renewable bound slack and its aggregate; the monotone branch of the settling rule (Section~4.8).
   \item A reproducibility note is new: single machine, single thread, bitwise replays, solver and library versions (Appendices~E.6 and~A.8).
